Falsifies `dma_validator::verification::an_accepted_descriptor_is_confined`.

The defect: `Desc::is_indirect` tests the wrong bit, so it answers false for every
descriptor. `check_descriptor` refuses an indirect descriptor because that is a table the
device would follow unchecked, outside anything the shadow ring copied; with this diff
the refusal never fires and a driver can hand the device an indirect table pointing
anywhere in physical memory.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(d.word & ((F_INDIRECT as u64) << 32), 0)`, on any
descriptor with the indirect flag set.

**Measured blind before milestone 211.** The harness used to assert `!d.is_indirect()`,
which is the predicate `check_descriptor` guards on, so the guard and the assertion were
one call and a defect in it satisfied both. Confirmed by applying this diff against the
old phrasing and watching it stay green. The flag's position in the descriptor word is
now written out. Its sibling assertion, the containment claim, is phrased through
`in_region`, and that predicate is pinned independently one harness up by
`in_region_is_sound`; the containment is restated over the addresses here anyway, so the
two claims stand on the same footing.

The record this replaces was the `F_INDIRECT` refusal deleted from `check_descriptor`.
The harness still catches that, and it is the more obvious of the two defects; it is
replaced rather than kept because a harness carries one record and the guard-drop was
never the blind spot.

diff --git a/crates/dma_validator/src/lib.rs b/crates/dma_validator/src/lib.rs
index 32d8aa80..97c00b8d 100644
--- a/crates/dma_validator/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/dma_validator/src/lib.rs
@@ -140,7 +140,7 @@ impl Desc {
     /// Whether `F_INDIRECT` is set: the buffer holds a table of further descriptors rather than
     /// data, which this validator refuses to chase (see the confinement decision above).
     pub const fn is_indirect(self) -> bool {
-        self.flags() & F_INDIRECT != 0
+        self.flags() & F_INDIRECT == F_INDIRECT << 1
     }
